            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through the weather stripping along the doors and windows, around window framing, and along the baseboards (between the floor and wall). 

I recommend you remove the trim and seal the framing members supporting the windows and doors. This can be done with either low expansion spray foam or interior grade caulking depending on the gap spacing observed. The weather stripping around doors and windows should be checked as well, if it is dry/brittle or broken, replace as required. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 100&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. In conjunction with a future upgrade to the exterior siding of this house, I have modelled adding 2 inches (R10) of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R10 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 430&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;17.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</mainWalls>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are aged wood framed ones, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all 14 windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 420&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;17.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



Please note, the above savings do not reflect the air sealing aspect of replacing windows and properly sealing the framing. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

If you switch to propane as a fuel source as noted above under the fure potential furnace upgrade, I have modelled replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,000&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy savings, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;0.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

&lt;b&gt;Note:&lt;/b&gt; Savings are based on national average hot water use. We expect savings to be 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

As a future upgrade possibility when your furnace is nearing it's end, I have modelled replacing your current oil fired furnace with an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 90&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy savings, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;34.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

&lt;b&gt;Note:&lt;/b&gt; Due to recent oil prices, savings are minimal when switching to propane, however historically propane has been a cheaper source of fuel. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



The furnace is currently setup as a 'whole house' ventilation system for your home, along with a fresh air supply, controlled by the humidistat located upstairs beside the temperature thermostat. If you find there is consistently excess humidity in the house, you will want to open the fresh air damper further. If you find the house is consistently becoming too dry, than you will want to close the fresh air damper further. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Woodstove and Air Distribution&lt;/b&gt;



Your furnace is currently setup with a secondary intake downstairs (close to the woodstove). With this secondary intake and the ventilation system described above, this will potentially allow you to distribute the heat from the woodstove around the home. By setting the humidistat knob to "ON", this should turn on only the furnace fan, drawing (and mixing) air  from both upstairs and downstairs, before re-distributing it around the home. 

Note that this will increase electrical consumption due to the increased use of the furnace fan. 



As the woodstove and furnace are fairly close, another "automated" option may be feasible. By installing a secondary thermostat downstairs and using the "cooling mode". When the woodstove creates an increase in temperature above the set-point, the furnace fan will turn on to distribute the heat, and turn-off when the temperature has dropped back to the set-point. 

I recommend having a contractor examine the feasibility of adding a secondary thermostat downstairs, if you wish to investigate an "automated" system. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your current woodstove for 50% of your heat load, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 140&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 

&lt;b&gt;Note:&lt;/b&gt; If you harvest your own wood, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,200&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Attic&lt;/b&gt;



During the evaluation, a cold spot was observed within the ceiling using an infrared camera. I recommend to check the insulation above the dining room and kitchen area (above the partition wall), to see if the insulation has been blown around from the wind or if moisture may have ruined the insulation. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.30 per kWh

Oil  - $0.84 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $6.58 per bag



